You'll pay $73 a month for EKS before a single container runs. ECS charges you nothing to exist. That one line settles more AWS architecture debates than any benchmark ever will.

Here's the thing people miss: 𝗘𝗖𝗦 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗘𝗞𝗦 𝗮𝗿𝗲𝗻'𝘁 𝗿𝗶𝘃𝗮𝗹𝘀 𝗶𝗻 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝘄𝗮𝘆 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝘁𝗵𝗶𝗻𝗸. They're both just orchestrators - they decide where your containers run. Fargate and EC2 decide what they run on. You can mix and match.
Quick history. ECS shipped in 2015 as AWS's own container orchestrator - celebrated ten years in 2025. Then Kubernetes ate the world, and in 2018 AWS launched EKS so teams could run real K8s without babysitting the control plane themselves.

𝗪𝗵𝗲𝗿𝗲 𝗘𝗖𝗦 𝘄𝗶𝗻𝘀:
No control plane fee. Native AWS APIs, so IAM, load balancers, and service discovery just work. A new engineer is productive in days, not weeks. The tradeoff: it's AWS-only, networking options are basic, and you're outside the CNCF tooling universe.

𝗪𝗵𝗲𝗿𝗲 𝗘𝗞𝗦 𝘄𝗶𝗻𝘀:
Standard Kubernetes APIs mean your manifests travel across clouds. Namespace-level multi-tenancy, fine-grained pod networking, and every operator in the ecosystem: Argo, Istio, Karpenter, Kubeflow. The tradeoff is real too: that $73/cluster fee multiplies fast (three clusters = $219/mo before workloads), and skilled K8s engineers cost more. One industry estimate pegs EKS operational overhead at roughly 5x the engineering hours of ECS.
The honest math: at small scale, ECS is cheaper and simpler. At large scale, that control plane fee becomes a rounding error and Karpenter bin-packing can cut compute 40–60%.

𝗠𝘆 𝗿𝘂𝗹𝗲 𝗼𝗳 𝘁𝗵𝘂𝗺𝗯: default to ECS + Fargate. Graduate to EKS when a real requirement: multi-cloud, K8s-native tooling, heavy multi-tenancy - forces your hand.
Compute costs, by the way, are identical between them. Fargate is Fargate on either side.

